edgesnap.library/--background--               edgesnap.library/--background--

   PURPOSE
        edgesnap.library snaps windows to the edges and corners of a
        screen, puts them back, lays them out in rectangles a client
        chooses, and lets the seam between two of them be dragged. It
        uses documented Intuition calls only, on AmigaOS 4, MorphOS and
        AROS, and patches nothing. The EdgeSnap commodity is its first
        client; any program can open the library and ask for the same
        things.

   FUNCTION
        OPENING THE LIBRARY

        Open "edgesnap.library" with version ES_API_VERSION, 2. The
        NAME line of every function below gives the revision that added
        it. OpenLibrary() compares the version only, and a 2.x too old
        for a call sends it into a jump table entry that is not there
        (on MorphOS a 68k illegal instruction at PC 0x4e, seen in the
        field on 2026-09-02). So a client checks lib_Revision of the
        base before it calls a function newer than 2.2, and accepts no
        library older than ES_LIB_MIN_REVISION.

        On AmigaOS 4 the functions are reached through the "main"
        interface, version 1, declared in <interfaces/edgesnap.h>:

            EdgeSnapBase = IExec->OpenLibrary("edgesnap.library",
                                              ES_API_VERSION);
            IEdgeSnap = (struct EdgeSnapIFace *)
                IExec->GetInterface(EdgeSnapBase, "main", 1, NULL);
            ...
            error = IEdgeSnap->ESnap_SnapWindow(win, ES_ZONE_LEFT);

        On MorphOS through the base and <ppcinline/edgesnap.h>, on AROS
        through the base and <proto/edgesnap.h>, with the registers the
        SYNOPSIS of each function gives.

        WINDOW REFERENCES

        Public entry points take a struct Window *, the lingua franca of
        every Intuition-facing API on these systems, with VALIDATE-PER-
        CALL semantics:

          - the pointer is used as an identity key and validated against
            the live window lists (under LockIBase) inside the call;
          - waits are followed by another validation before the window
            is read or used again; Intuition calls use the validated
            pointer just after the lock is released;
          - the library NEVER dereferences a caller-supplied window
            outside that validated section, and NEVER retains it beyond
            the call, except as an opaque key in the snap registry;
          - a window that no longer exists yields ES_ERR_STALE, never a
            crash. Callers must treat any window as free to vanish at
            any moment - on Amiga systems it is;
          - registry state defends against pointer reuse (a new window
            allocated at a dead window's address): restore only acts if
            the window still sits where the snap put it, otherwise the
            state is dropped with ES_ERR_CHANGED. An inherited-state
            mishap is thereby bounded to "nothing happens", never "a
            stranger window moves".

        A client that keeps state for longer than a call names windows
        by serial instead (ESnap_QueryWindowSerial).

        CONTEXT

        All functions must be called from a normal application task
        context. None of them may be called from interrupts, input
        handlers, or any code running in input.device context. Snaps and
        restores can wait for a glide and, on AROS, for repainting;
        ESnap_FeedInput can also wait for a drop. Internal locking uses
        LockIBase and the registry semaphore. Geometry changes are
        requested asynchronously (ChangeWindowBox semantics): a
        successful return means "accepted", not "already applied".

        OWNERSHIP

        The library never takes ownership of caller memory: windows stay
        the application's, tag lists are read during the call only, and
        no function returns library-owned pointers. Results come back
        through caller-provided storage.

        ERRORS

        Every function returns ES_OK or one negative ES_ERR_* code. The
        distinctions matter and are part of the contract:

          ES_ERR_UNSUPPORTED  the capability does not exist here (system,
                              screen); asking again will not help until
                              the environment changes.
          ES_ERR_REJECTED     the capability exists but policy refuses
                              this window (excluded, unsnappable,
                              backdrop, borderless, in a group locked for
                              the caller...).
          ES_ERR_STALE        the referenced window, or id, no longer
                              exists. Never fatal: callers must expect
                              windows to vanish at any moment.
          ES_ERR_NOT_SNAPPED  no snap state is recorded for this window.
          ES_ERR_CHANGED      recorded state was dropped because the
                              window's geometry was changed independently
                              (by the application or the user).
          ES_ERR_NO_MEMORY    a table the library keeps is full; nothing
                              was done.
          ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS     an argument is missing or out of range;
                              nothing was done.
          ES_ERR_IN_USE       a role only one client may hold is held
                              (2.10).

        VERSIONING

        From the first public release on:

          - existing error values, zone values, capability bits, tag
            values and function signatures never change meaning and are
            never reused;
          - new capabilities arrive as new bits, new options as new
            tags; both are discoverable, and unknown tags are ignored;
          - new functions are appended to the jump table and the
            interface, never moved, and the revision says which exist;
          - clients state the API generation they were built against by
            opening the library with the matching version.

   SEE ALSO
        --frontend--, --dividers--, --tokens--, edgesnap_types.h

edgesnap.library/--dividers--                   edgesnap.library/--dividers--

   PURPOSE
        When two windows are snapped side by side, the seam between them
        is a handle: dragging it resizes both, so a half/half split
        becomes 60/40 without touching either window's size gadget. The
        library finds the seam and performs the resize; a frontend puts a
        thin window with a resize pointer on top of it, and reports the
        drags.

   FUNCTION
        A seam is a LINE, not a pair (2.3): it may join more than two
        windows, and a layout may hold several. One window facing two
        stacked ones has a vertical seam moving all three and a
        horizontal seam moving only the two, and four quadrants have one
        of each.

        From 2.24 ESnap_QueryDivider, ESnap_QueryDividerAt,
        ESnap_MoveDivider and ESnap_MoveDividerAt look at the front
        screen only, and take in the windows of the layout groups on it
        (2.12), those placed without a registry slot included. A seam
        between two windows of a group keeps the group's gap when it is
        moved, and a point in that gap is not covered by a window that
        lies behind the windows beside it. A seam of a group locked for
        the caller is never reported and never moved. QueryDivider and
        MoveDivider still take a seam between windows of no group first,
        when there is one. A group's seam can report a position outside
        minPosition..maxPosition (see ESnap_QueryDividers): a move
        clamps, so a frontend should move nothing on a click without a
        drag.

        MoveDivider and MoveDividerAt keep the contracts of 2.2 and 2.3
        and do not ask whether a window was moved by hand: on the seam of
        a group that is not locked for the caller they put such a window
        back in its place beside the seam, where ESnap_MoveDividerT
        answers ES_ERR_CHANGED. QueryDivider and QueryDividerAt report no
        seam there, so the commodity does not offer it to the user, but
        any task may call the two: a tiler that wants its windows left
        alone locks its group.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryDivider(), ESnap_QueryDividerAt(), ESnap_MoveDivider(),
        ESnap_MoveDividerAt(), --tokens--

edgesnap.library/--frontend--                   edgesnap.library/--frontend--

   PURPOSE
        The entry points that a FRONTEND needs - EdgeSnap's own
        commodity, or any alternative one. An application that just
        wants to place its windows never touches them.

   FUNCTION
        The division of labour: the frontend owns the input handler (a
        commodities CxCustom object, which must only count events and
        signal its task - never call Intuition) and the drawing of the
        preview frame, because that is platform decoration. Everything
        that decides or remembers stays in the library. The frontend
        hands over raw event counts with ESnap_FeedInput and the mouse's
        own travel with ESnap_FeedMotion, and gets back a report of what
        happened; a library never prints, so the frontend logs on its
        behalf.

        A frontend that runs beside another client says so with
        ESnap_RegisterClient and ES_CL_ENGINE: from then on the engine
        takes its input from that task only.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_FeedInput(), ESnap_FeedMotion(), ESnap_ResetInput(),
        ESnap_IgnoreWindows(), ESnap_QueryScreenArea(),
        ESnap_RegisterClient(), --dividers--

edgesnap.library/--tokens--                       edgesnap.library/--tokens--

   PURPOSE
        A tiler's own seams. ESnap_QueryDividers lists the seams between
        the windows of a group, each with a token; ESnap_MoveDividerT
        moves the seam a token names; ESnap_QueryDividerWindows says
        which windows those are.

   FUNCTION
        A token names the windows on each side of a seam, not a line: it
        moves them and no other, and stays valid across its own moves for
        as long as they are that seam. Once they are not (one of them
        closed or left the group, or a window of the group joined them
        across the seam) it is retired, and a retired token is never
        valid again; the next QueryDividers gives the seam they make now
        a token of its own. Tokens are never 0. Any task may ask
        QueryDividers and QueryDividerWindows, and either retires a token
        it finds no longer valid, whoever asks: a stale token is stale
        for everyone. MoveDividerT alone refuses a caller the group is
        locked for, and does so before it checks the token's windows.

        The seams are found from the boxes EdgeSnap last put the windows
        in (a placement, a snap, UnsnapWindow, a divider move), so a
        tiler may ask right after a placement it has not seen land, and a
        window EdgeSnap asked to move in the last two seconds is never
        counted as moved by hand (ES_DIF_MOVED, ES_ERR_CHANGED), whatever
        box it shows on its way. A window still in the box it had before
        EdgeSnap's last request, or in the box EdgeSnap last put it in,
        is not counted as moved by hand, however long after the request.
        Once it is seen in the requested box the earlier box stops
        counting. From 2.25 a window joining before its remembered
        placement has landed gets the transit interval on joining. A
        window not placed since it joined counts where EdgeSnap last put
        it, if UnsnapWindow can undo that (a snap, or a placement or move
        of a window with a way back), or else where it stood when it
        joined: an ES_PF_NO_RESTORE placement of a window with no way
        back leaves nothing to remember, so a tiler that places with it
        adds its windows to the group first. Members that are excluded or
        cannot be snapped are on no seam.

        Windows of the group face each other across a seam when the gap
        between them is the group's (ESnap_SetGroupOptionsA), and a move
        keeps it. A change of the gap moves no window, and the boxes
        EdgeSnap put them in keep the old one: a seam between them may
        not be found until the tiler places them again. position is the
        cell boundary ES_PF_CELL starts from: the windows left of (above)
        the seam end at position less half the gap, rounded up (x plus
        width, y plus height), and those right of (below) it start at
        position plus half the gap, rounded down. For an odd gap it lies
        half a pixel right of (below) the middle of the gap.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryDividers(), ESnap_MoveDividerT(),
        ESnap_QueryDividerWindows(), --dividers--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_CreateGroup         edgesnap.library/ESnap_CreateGroup

   NAME
        ESnap_CreateGroup -- make a layout group (V2.12)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_CreateGroup(workArea, name, flags, group_out)
        D0                        D0        A0    D1     A1

        LONG ESnap_CreateGroup(ULONG workArea, const char *name, ULONG flags,
                               ULONG *group_out);

   FUNCTION
        A group is a tiler's unit of state: these windows, in this work
        area, owned by this client. The library keeps the membership, by
        window serial, so that the interactive engine can tell a window a
        tiler owns from one it may snap. The caller must hold
        ES_CL_LAYOUT, that is what the role is for.

   INPUTS
        workArea  - an id from ESnap_QueryWorkAreas, or 0 for "wherever"
        name      - for logs, or NULL
        flags     - ES_GF_LOCKED or 0
        group_out - receives the group's id

   FLAGS
        ES_GF_LOCKED  keep everyone else off these windows. The owner's
                      own SnapWindow, PlaceWindow and PlaceWindowsA work
                      as usual; from any other task, the commodity
                      included, they answer ES_ERR_REJECTED, and the
                      interactive engine does not snap a locked window
                      when the user drags it. It does count the touch:
                      ESnap_QueryGeneration moves, so the owner learns
                      that the user reached for its window and can decide
                      what that means. A locked group whose owner
                      switched its layout off (ESnap_Enable(FALSE) from a
                      layout client) keeps nobody out until it switches
                      back on.

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS / ES_ERR_REJECTED (no layout
                role) / ES_ERR_STALE (no such work area) /
                ES_ERR_NO_MEMORY (sixteen groups).

   NOTES
        A group dies with the client that made it, when that client
        unregisters or its task is gone. Group ids are never reused.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_DeleteGroup(), ESnap_GroupAddWindow(),
        ESnap_SetGroupOptionsA(), ESnap_RegisterClient()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_DeleteGroup         edgesnap.library/ESnap_DeleteGroup

   NAME
        ESnap_DeleteGroup -- remove a layout group (V2.12)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_DeleteGroup(group)
        D0                        D0

        LONG ESnap_DeleteGroup(ULONG group);

   FUNCTION
        Remove a group. Its windows stay where they are and belong to no
        group.

   INPUTS
        group - the group's id

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_REJECTED (not the owner's task) /
                ES_ERR_STALE (no such group).

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_CreateGroup()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_Enable                   edgesnap.library/ESnap_Enable

   NAME
        ESnap_Enable -- switch the drag engine, or a layout, on or off (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_Enable(on)
        D0                   D0

        LONG ESnap_Enable(BOOL on);

   FUNCTION
        The master switch for the interactive engine: drag detection and
        hotkeys. Programmatic calls keep working while it is off.

   INPUTS
        on - TRUE to switch on, FALSE to switch off

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK.

   NOTES
        From 2.10 what it switches depends on the caller's role. From the
        holder of ES_CL_ENGINE it is the engine's switch. From a client
        that holds ES_CL_LAYOUT only, it switches that client's own
        layout and leaves the engine alone: a locked group of a client
        whose layout is off keeps nobody out until it is switched back
        on. A caller that never registered moves the engine's switch, as
        in 2.2, so a client written before roles existed does not change
        meaning.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_RegisterClient(), ESnap_CreateGroup()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_ExcludeWindow     edgesnap.library/ESnap_ExcludeWindow

   NAME
        ESnap_ExcludeWindow -- keep EdgeSnap off a window, or let it back (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_ExcludeWindow(win, exclude)
        D0                          A0   D0

        LONG ESnap_ExcludeWindow(struct Window *win, BOOL exclude);

   FUNCTION
        Exclude (TRUE) or re-include (FALSE) win from every EdgeSnap
        behavior: drag snapping, hotkeys, programmatic ESnap_SnapWindow.

   INPUTS
        win     - the window
        exclude - TRUE to exclude it, FALSE to include it again

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_STALE / ES_ERR_NO_MEMORY (no room left, or
                the library already tells apart as many windows as it
                can).

   NOTES
        From 2.27 the exclusion belongs to the window, by the identity of
        ESnap_QueryWindowSerial, and is dropped with it. A window opened
        where an excluded one was is not excluded, provided the library
        saw the closed one gone first: the engine looks with every input
        it is fed, so any pointer movement in between is enough. What the
        library cannot see from outside Intuition is a window closed and
        another opened at once, with nothing in between: the system gives
        the new one the same memory, window and layer, and it inherits
        the exclusion. In practice only a program reopening its own
        window does that, and it should re-include the window before
        closing it if that matters. Up to 2.26 the exclusion was held by
        address, and a window opened where an excluded one had been could
        inherit it at any later time.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_SnapWindow(), ESnap_QueryWindowSerial()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_FeedInput             edgesnap.library/ESnap_FeedInput

   NAME
        ESnap_FeedInput -- hand the engine the input since the last call (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        ESnap_FeedInput(presses, motions, releases, qualifiers, report)
                        D0       D1       D2        D3          A0

        void ESnap_FeedInput(ULONG presses, ULONG motions, ULONG releases,
                             ULONG qualifiers, struct ESnapReport *report);

   FUNCTION
        Feed the engine the events seen since the last call: how many
        presses, pointer motions and releases arrived (counts, so a fast
        press+release pair is never lost) and the qualifier bits that
        came with the last mouse event. Fills *report; never fails.

   INPUTS
        presses    - mouse button presses since the last call
        motions    - pointer motions since the last call
        releases   - mouse button releases since the last call
        qualifiers - the qualifier bits of the last mouse event
        report     - where the outcome goes

   RESULT
        None. *report says what happened: a drag recognised, the zone
        under the pointer, a preview frame to show or erase, a snap and
        its result.

   NOTES
        From 2.10, while a client holds ES_CL_ENGINE, calls from any
        other task are ignored.

        From 2.24 a release outside every zone can give a window EdgeSnap
        snapped its size back (ES_OPT_DragRestore). The report does not
        say so, since it does not grow: ESnap_QueryWindow answers
        ES_ZONE_NONE for that window afterwards. Such a release can wait
        up to ten ticks for Intuition to put the window where the drag
        left it, then up to nineteen ticks for the glide when
        ES_OPT_Animate is on. At 50 Hz these are one fifth of a second
        and 0.38 seconds respectively. On AROS a move and resize can add
        two ticks, and a Wanderer window can also take up to four box or
        repaint waits, each capped at 150 ticks (three seconds at 50 Hz).
        These bounds count the explicit waits; the system calls and
        scheduling take their own time.

        From 2.25 the decision compares the pointer's distance from its
        start with the threshold set by ES_OPT_DragMinPx, in either axis.
        Size rounding slack is not a drag threshold. If the live box is
        still within that threshold of its start, the restore uses the
        pointer sampled with it, so a window kept on screen restores
        under the pointer too. Otherwise the live box and the grab's
        offset locate the drop, even when a last motion was batched with
        the release or the pointer went on after it. A window that
        followed the pointer also qualifies when the sampled press missed
        its bar; its grab comes from the latest motion's box and pointer,
        sampled together. The original bar-press grab still serves
        outline drags. Only the release's own qualifiers decide whether
        the bypass key skips the restore. A snap still uses the zone the
        preview showed.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_FeedMotion(), ESnap_ResetInput(), ESnap_SetOptionsA(),
        --frontend--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_FeedMotion           edgesnap.library/ESnap_FeedMotion

   NAME
        ESnap_FeedMotion -- hand the engine the mouse's own travel (V2.7)

   SYNOPSIS
        ESnap_FeedMotion(dx, dy)
                         D0  D1

        void ESnap_FeedMotion(LONG dx, LONG dy);

   FUNCTION
        Where Intuition keeps a window inside the screen (MorphOS as
        delivered, AROS with "offscreen move" switched off), a window
        dragged against the edge stops there, and the pointer stays
        pinned where it grabbed the title bar: it never touches the edge,
        so no zone would ever be entered. The mouse keeps moving all the
        same, and the frontend's input handler sees that travel. This
        call reports it, accumulated since the last ESnap_FeedInput: the
        engine then treats a pointer that stands still while the travel
        keeps pushing against an edge the window is flush with as being
        on that edge.

   INPUTS
        dx, dy - the travel, in pixels and signed

   RESULT
        None.

   NOTES
        A frontend that never calls it loses nothing but this case.
        From 2.10, while a client holds ES_CL_ENGINE, calls from any
        other task are ignored. ES_OPT_PushPx (2.26) sets how much travel
        counts.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_FeedInput(), ESnap_SetOptionsA(), --frontend--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_FindWindow           edgesnap.library/ESnap_FindWindow

   NAME
        ESnap_FindWindow -- the window behind a serial (V2.6)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_FindWindow(serial, window)
        D0                       D0      A0

        LONG ESnap_FindWindow(ULONG serial, struct Window **window);

   FUNCTION
        The window behind a serial, validated against the live window
        lists.

   INPUTS
        serial - a serial from ESnap_QueryWindowSerial or
                 ESnap_QueryWindows
        window - receives the window

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_STALE once the window is gone, and the
                serial is retired with it.

   NOTES
        The result has the same validate-per-call rule as every other
        window pointer here: it is good for the next call, not for
        keeping.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryWindowSerial()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_GroupAddWindow   edgesnap.library/ESnap_GroupAddWindow

   NAME
        ESnap_GroupAddWindow -- put a window in a group (V2.12)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_GroupAddWindow(group, win)
        D0                           D0     A0

        LONG ESnap_GroupAddWindow(ULONG group, struct Window *win);

   FUNCTION
        Make win a member of group.

   INPUTS
        group - the group's id
        win   - the window

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK, also when win is already in this group /
                ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS (win NULL) / ES_ERR_REJECTED (not the
                owner's task, or the window is in another group) /
                ES_ERR_STALE (no such group or window) /
                ES_ERR_NO_MEMORY (the group is full).

   NOTES
        A window belongs to one group at a time: adding it to a second
        one is ES_ERR_REJECTED, removing it first is the way. Thirty-two
        windows per group. A window that closes leaves its group by
        itself.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_GroupRemoveWindow(), ESnap_QueryGroupOf()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_GroupRemoveWindow

   NAME
        ESnap_GroupRemoveWindow -- take a window out of its group (V2.12)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_GroupRemoveWindow(group, win)
        D0                              D0     A0

        LONG ESnap_GroupRemoveWindow(ULONG group, struct Window *win);

   FUNCTION
        Take win out of group.

   INPUTS
        group - the group's id
        win   - the window

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS (win NULL) / ES_ERR_REJECTED (not
                the owner's task) / ES_ERR_STALE (no such group, or the
                window is not in it).

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_GroupAddWindow()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_IgnoreWindows     edgesnap.library/ESnap_IgnoreWindows

   NAME
        ESnap_IgnoreWindows -- windows the dock scan must skip (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_IgnoreWindows(windows, count)
        D0                          A0       D0

        LONG ESnap_IgnoreWindows(struct Window **windows, ULONG count);

   FUNCTION
        Windows the dock scan must never mistake for a panel: a
        frontend's own preview frame is thin, edge-flush and long, which
        is exactly a dock's shape. Each call replaces the previous list.

   INPUTS
        windows - an array of window pointers, or NULL to clear
        count   - how many there are, or 0 to clear

   RESULT
        error - always ES_OK.

   NOTES
        Six windows are kept; any beyond the sixth are not ignored.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryScreenArea(), --frontend--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_MoveDivider         edgesnap.library/ESnap_MoveDivider

   NAME
        ESnap_MoveDivider -- drag the first divider (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_MoveDivider(position)
        D0                        D0

        LONG ESnap_MoveDivider(LONG position);

   FUNCTION
        Drag the FIRST divider in the layout to a screen coordinate.
        With more than one seam on screen "the first" is whichever the
        layout walk finds first, so new code should say which seam it
        means and call ESnap_MoveDividerAt().

   INPUTS
        position - where the seam should go, in screen coordinates

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_STALE (a window on the seam is gone) /
                ES_ERR_UNSUPPORTED (no seam).

   NOTES
        Kept with the arguments it had in 2.2, because a library's major
        version is its compatibility promise and a 2.x client must keep
        working on every later 2.x: this vector is neither moved nor
        changed.

        From 2.25 position is clamped to -32768..32767 first, then to
        the seam's limits.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_MoveDividerAt(), ESnap_QueryDivider(), --dividers--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_MoveDividerAt     edgesnap.library/ESnap_MoveDividerAt

   NAME
        ESnap_MoveDividerAt -- drag the divider on a given line (V2.4)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_MoveDividerAt(vertical, line, position)
        D0                          A0        A1    D0

        LONG ESnap_MoveDividerAt(LONG vertical, LONG line, LONG position);

   FUNCTION
        Drag a NAMED divider to a screen coordinate: every window on it
        is resized, and the value is clamped so none can be squeezed
        away. Window references are re-validated first, as everywhere
        else.

        The seam is named by the line it sits on - vertical and the
        coordinate a query reported as position - because a layout can
        hold more than one and "the divider" would be ambiguous. A seam
        that has stopped existing is not moved.

   INPUTS
        vertical - 1 for a seam dragged left and right, 0 for up and down
        line     - the position a query reported for the seam
        position - where the seam should go, in screen coordinates

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_STALE (a window on the seam is gone) /
                ES_ERR_UNSUPPORTED (no seam on that line).

   NOTES
        From 2.25 position is clamped to -32768..32767 before the seam's
        own limits are applied.

        From 2.24 MoveDividerAt follows the seam ESnap_QueryDividerAt
        last reported to the calling task (an answer of no seam leaves
        it as it was), or the one the task last moved itself: a line
        within twelve pixels of where that seam was, or the line the task
        last asked it to move to, moves that seam or none (ES_ERR_STALE
        when one of its windows is gone, ES_ERR_UNSUPPORTED when they are
        no longer that seam on the front screen), never another seam that
        has come to lie there. For windows of a group, that seam is the
        one of exactly those windows; for windows of no group, it is the
        seam on that line whose sides hold them all, windows that have
        joined it since included. Any other line takes the nearest seam
        on that axis, as before. A frontend that asks about other points
        while it shows a seam moves the seam of the last answer it got,
        so that is the one it should show.

        From 2.25 an ES_ERR_STALE answer keeps the record, marked gone:
        every later call that follows it answers ES_ERR_STALE and moves
        nothing. A QueryDividerAt answer of a seam replaces it; a move on
        a line it does not follow drops it and takes the nearest seam as
        before. The records of eight tasks are kept at a time. A task
        that is gone gives way first; otherwise the record made longest
        ago is evicted, and that task's next move takes the nearest seam
        until it records one again.

        Revision 2.3 changed ESnap_MoveDivider's arguments in place, which
        was a mistake and lived only on the author's machines; 2.4 put
        them back and appended this function instead.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryDividerAt(), ESnap_MoveDividerT(), --dividers--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_MoveDividerT       edgesnap.library/ESnap_MoveDividerT

   NAME
        ESnap_MoveDividerT -- drag the seam a token names (V2.24)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_MoveDividerT(token, position, flags, applied)
        D0                         D0     D1        D2     A0

        LONG ESnap_MoveDividerT(ULONG token, LONG position, ULONG flags,
                                LONG *applied);

   FUNCTION
        Move the seam of token to position, clamped to its limits. Only
        the token's windows move, and the token stays valid. Geometry
        changes are requested, not applied (ChangeWindowBox semantics).

   INPUTS
        token    - from ESnap_QueryDividers
        position - where the seam should go, in screen coordinates
        flags    - ES_PF_KEEP_ZORDER, the windows are not raised (see
                   ESnap_PlaceWindow), or 0
        applied  - receives the position it was moved to, on ES_OK only;
                   may be NULL

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS (token 0, or an unknown flag) /
                ES_ERR_REJECTED (the group is locked for the caller; the
                token is not touched) / ES_ERR_STALE (no such token, or
                its windows are no longer that seam: it is retired) /
                ES_ERR_CHANGED (a window moved by hand, outside the
                current and earlier boxes and the transit interval
                described in --tokens--; the token stays) /
                ES_ERR_UNSUPPORTED (no room to move it after every
                window's limits; the token stays).

   NOTES
        From 2.25 position is clamped to -32768..32767 first. After the
        argument and group lock checks, the order from 2.25 is: still
        that seam, moved by hand, room to move, then the move.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryDividers(), ESnap_MoveDividerAt(), --tokens--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_PlaceWindow         edgesnap.library/ESnap_PlaceWindow

   NAME
        ESnap_PlaceWindow -- put a window in a rectangle (V2.5)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_PlaceWindow(win, rect, flags)
        D0                        A0   A1    D0

        LONG ESnap_PlaceWindow(struct Window *win,
                               const struct ESnapRect *rect, ULONG flags);

   FUNCTION
        Put win into rect with the contract of ESnap_SnapWindow: the
        geometry before placement is recorded for ESnap_UnsnapWindow, the
        window's size limits are honoured with the result anchored to the
        edge of rect that touches the usable area, and the errors are the
        same. Geometry changes are requested, not applied
        (ChangeWindowBox semantics).

   INPUTS
        win   - the window
        rect  - the rectangle, in screen coordinates
        flags - ES_PF_* below, or 0

   FLAGS
        ES_PF_NO_RESTORE   spend no registry slot on a window that was
                           never adopted. A window already in the
                           registry is followed all the same, so
                           UnsnapWindow still gives back the geometry from
                           before the first placement. For a client that
                           moves windows many times a minute.
        ES_PF_KEEP_ZORDER  do not reorder the windows. On AROS a window
                           is brought to the front before it is moved, so
                           that the move copies everything and leaves no
                           damage a file manager will not repaint; with
                           this flag it stays where it is in the stack,
                           damage and all.
        ES_PF_CELL         (V2.20) rect is a cell of the layout of the
                           window's group: the group's margins and gap
                           are taken off it (see ESnap_SetGroupOptionsA).
                           A window in no group is placed in the cell as
                           it is. An older library answers
                           ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS.

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS / ES_ERR_STALE / ES_ERR_REJECTED /
                ES_ERR_NO_MEMORY.

   NOTES
        ESnap_QueryWindow reports ES_ZONE_RECT for a window placed this
        way. Placement is never animated.

        From 2.25 rect's x and y must lie in -32768..32767, and its width
        and height in 1..32767, as Intuition can represent them;
        otherwise ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS, before fitting the window's limits or
        a cell.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_PlaceWindowsA(), ESnap_SnapWindow(), ESnap_UnsnapWindow(),
        ESnap_SetGroupOptionsA()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_PlaceWindowsA     edgesnap.library/ESnap_PlaceWindowsA

   NAME
        ESnap_PlaceWindowsA -- put several windows in their rectangles (V2.5)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_PlaceWindowsA(list, count, flags)
        D0                          A0    D0     D1

        LONG ESnap_PlaceWindowsA(struct ESnapPlacement *list, ULONG count,
                                 ULONG flags);

   FUNCTION
        A whole layout in one call, each entry placed as
        ESnap_PlaceWindow would place it. Every entry gets its own
        result, so one stale window does not fail the rest. The library
        orders the moves so that windows which shrink go first and
        windows which grow go last; it cannot make the batch atomic, each
        move is a request of its own.

   INPUTS
        list  - the windows and their rectangles; result is filled in
        count - how many entries, 1..ES_PLACE_MAX
        flags - ES_PF_*, as for ESnap_PlaceWindow, for every entry

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK once every entry has been tried; ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS
                for a NULL list, an empty one, or more than ES_PLACE_MAX
                entries.

   NOTES
        From 2.25 the rectangle bounds of ESnap_PlaceWindow apply per
        entry: a bad rectangle receives ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS in that entry's
        result and moves nothing, while the other entries are tried as
        usual.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_PlaceWindow(), ESnap_QueryWindows()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryCapabilities

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryCapabilities -- what this environment can do (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        caps = ESnap_QueryCapabilities()
        D0

        ULONG ESnap_QueryCapabilities(void);

   FUNCTION
        What this environment can do right now (screen, compositing,
        build). Capabilities can change when the environment does (a
        screen switch): query, do not cache forever.

   RESULT
        caps - a mask of ES_CAP_* bits. Never fails.

   NOTES
        Bits are added, never reused. Libraries up to 2.27 report
        ES_CAP_SNAP, ES_CAP_RESTORE, ES_CAP_DRAG_DETECT,
        ES_CAP_PREVIEW_OUTLINE, ES_CAP_HOTKEYS and ES_CAP_GUTTER (the
        divider). ES_CAP_PREVIEW_ALPHA stays out: a translucent preview
        needs compositing and is not implemented.

   SEE ALSO
        edgesnap_types.h

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryDivider       edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryDivider

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryDivider -- where the divider is, if any (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryDivider(thickness, divider)
        D0                         D0         A0

        LONG ESnap_QueryDivider(ULONG thickness,
                                struct ESnapDivider *divider);

   FUNCTION
        Where the divider is, if there is one: the first seam the layout
        walk finds on the front screen.

   INPUTS
        thickness - how wide the handle should be
        divider   - filled in, with present = 0 when there is no pair

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS.

   NOTES
        From 2.24 the strip of a seam between windows of a group whose
        gap is wider than thickness is the gap itself, so a handle put on
        it fills the space between the two windows.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryDividerAt(), ESnap_MoveDivider(), --dividers--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryDividerAt   edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryDividerAt

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryDividerAt -- the seam nearest a point (V2.3)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryDividerAt(thickness, x, y, divider)
        D0                           D0         D1 D2 A0

        LONG ESnap_QueryDividerAt(ULONG thickness, LONG x, LONG y,
                                  struct ESnapDivider *divider);

   FUNCTION
        The divider nearest a point, which is how a frontend asks "what
        is the pointer on?".

   INPUTS
        thickness - how wide the handle should be
        x, y      - the point, in screen coordinates
        divider   - filled in; present is 0 when nothing is within reach

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS.

   NOTES
        From 2.16 a point ON a seam, within a few pixels of its strip,
        also answers whether the seam can be grabbed there: when another
        window covers it at that point, present is 0. Asked from further
        away, the nearest seam is named as before.

        From 2.24 the answer is remembered for the calling task, and
        ESnap_MoveDividerAt follows that seam.

        From 2.25 a point with x or y outside -32768..32767 reports no
        seam.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_MoveDividerAt(), ESnap_QueryDivider(), --dividers--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryDividerWindows

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryDividerWindows -- the windows on a token's seam (V2.24)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryDividerWindows(token, serials, count, needed)
        D0                                D0     A0       D1     A1

        LONG ESnap_QueryDividerWindows(ULONG token, ULONG *serials,
                                       ULONG count, ULONG *needed);

   FUNCTION
        The serials of the token's windows, those left of (above) the
        seam, then those right of (below) it, each side top-most (along a
        vertical seam) or left-most first: serials[0] is serialA,
        serials[countA] is serialB. Up to count are written, and *needed
        receives how many there are.

   INPUTS
        token   - from ESnap_QueryDividers
        serials - where the serials go
        count   - how many serials there is room for
        needed  - receives how many there are

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS (needed NULL, serials NULL with a
                count, or token 0) / ES_ERR_STALE (no such token, or its
                windows are no longer that seam: it is retired) /
                ES_ERR_UNSUPPORTED.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryDividers(), ESnap_FindWindow(), --tokens--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryDividers     edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryDividers

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryDividers -- the seams of a group, with tokens (V2.24)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryDividers(group, buf, count, needed)
        D0                          D0     A0   D1     A1

        LONG ESnap_QueryDividers(ULONG group, struct ESnapDividerInfo *buf,
                                 ULONG count, ULONG *needed);

   FUNCTION
        The seams between the windows of a group. Up to count entries are
        written to buf, vertical seams first, then horizontal ones, each
        by position, then by from; *needed receives how many there are in
        all, so a short buffer shows as *needed > count. from and to are
        where along the seam its windows face each other, to one past the
        last pixel (y plus height along a vertical seam, x plus width
        along a horizontal one). The limits honour every window's own
        size limits.

   INPUTS
        group  - the group's id
        buf    - where the entries go
        count  - how many entries buf has room for
        needed - receives how many seams there are

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS (needed NULL, or buf NULL with a
                count) / ES_ERR_STALE (no such group) /
                ES_ERR_UNSUPPORTED.

   NOTES
        position can lie outside the limits, in a layout where a window's
        own limits kept it from filling its cell: a move clamps, so
        moving to position may then move the windows.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_MoveDividerT(), ESnap_QueryDividerWindows(), --tokens--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryGeneration

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryGeneration -- a counter of window changes (V2.5)

   SYNOPSIS
        generation = ESnap_QueryGeneration(screen)
        D0                                 A0

        ULONG ESnap_QueryGeneration(struct Screen *screen);

   FUNCTION
        A counter that moves whenever the window set or any window's
        geometry changes. A client polls it and re-reads the list only
        when the number moved.

   INPUTS
        screen - narrows nothing yet: the generation counts changes on
                 every screen

   RESULT
        generation - the counter.

   NOTES
        The library does not see windows open or close, it validates per
        call, so this call rescans the window lists each time: cheap for
        a desktop's worth of windows, and the caller's poll is what pays
        for it.

        From 2.12 it also moves when the user drags a window of a locked
        group, so the group's owner learns that the user reached for it.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryWindows(), ESnap_CreateGroup()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryGroupOf       edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryGroupOf

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryGroupOf -- the group a window belongs to (V2.12)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryGroupOf(win, group_out)
        D0                         A0   A1

        LONG ESnap_QueryGroupOf(struct Window *win, ULONG *group_out);

   FUNCTION
        The group of win, if it has one. Any task may ask.

   INPUTS
        win       - the window
        group_out - receives the group's id, or 0

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS / ES_ERR_STALE.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_GroupAddWindow(), ESnap_CreateGroup()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryScreenArea

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryScreenArea -- where snapping may place windows (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryScreenArea(screen, area)
        D0                            A0      A1

        LONG ESnap_QueryScreenArea(struct Screen *screen,
                                   struct ESnapArea *area);

   FUNCTION
        Where snapping may place windows on this screen, and what was
        reserved by docks and margins. Useful to a frontend's
        diagnostics, and to any client that wants to lay windows out
        itself.

   INPUTS
        screen - the screen to ask about
        area   - where the usable rectangle and the insets go

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryWorkAreas(), ESnap_SetOptionsA()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryWindow         edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryWindow

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryWindow -- the zone a window is snapped to (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryWindow(win, zone_out)
        D0                        A0   A1

        LONG ESnap_QueryWindow(struct Window *win, ULONG *zone_out);

   FUNCTION
        The current snap zone of win.

   INPUTS
        win      - the window
        zone_out - receives ES_ZONE_*, ES_ZONE_NONE when not snapped

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS / ES_ERR_STALE.

   NOTES
        From 2.5 a window placed by ESnap_PlaceWindow reports
        ES_ZONE_RECT.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_SnapWindow(), ESnap_QueryWindows()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryWindowSerial

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryWindowSerial -- the serial that names a window (V2.6)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryWindowSerial(win, serial)
        D0                              A0   A1

        LONG ESnap_QueryWindowSerial(struct Window *win, ULONG *serial);

   FUNCTION
        A struct Window * is an address, and an address is reused: a
        client that keeps state for a whole session, not just for a drag,
        meets that as the normal case. So every window the library
        observes gets a serial, never 0 and never reused, and the one
        promise is: same serial, same window.

        Observed means: seen by ESnap_QueryWindows or
        ESnap_QueryGeneration, or asked about with this call. A window
        closed and another opened at the same address is told apart by
        its layer; a window never observed has no serial yet, and gets
        one when it is. What the library cannot see is a window that came
        and went entirely between two observations, and a client that
        polls ESnap_QueryGeneration sees often.

        This call gives the serial of win, assigned now if it had none.

   INPUTS
        win    - the window
        serial - receives its serial

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_STALE (no such window).

   NOTES
        A window closed and another opened at once gets the same memory
        back, window and layer, and keeps the closed one's serial unless
        the library saw the close: from 2.27 the engine looks with every
        input it is fed, so it is enough that the pointer moves in
        between.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_FindWindow(), ESnap_QueryWindows()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryWindows       edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryWindows

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryWindows -- the windows of a screen (V2.5)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryWindows(screen, buf, count, needed)
        D0                         A0      A1   D0     A2

        LONG ESnap_QueryWindows(struct Screen *screen,
                                struct ESnapWindowInfo *buf, ULONG count,
                                ULONG *needed);

   FUNCTION
        The windows as the library sees them, for a client that lays
        them out rather than snapping one at a time (a tiler), in
        Intuition's front-to-back order. Up to count entries are written
        to buf; *needed receives how many there are in all, so a short
        buffer shows as *needed > count and the caller can come back
        with a bigger one.

   INPUTS
        screen - the screen, or NULL for the frontmost
        buf    - where the entries go
        count  - how many entries buf has room for
        needed - receives how many windows there are

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK, or ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS when needed is NULL, or buf is
                NULL with a count.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryGeneration(), ESnap_QueryWindowSerial(),
        ESnap_PlaceWindowsA()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryWorkAreas   edgesnap.library/ESnap_QueryWorkAreas

   NAME
        ESnap_QueryWorkAreas -- the work areas of a screen, with ids (V2.11)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_QueryWorkAreas(screen, buf, count, needed)
        D0                           A0      A1   D0     A2

        LONG ESnap_QueryWorkAreas(struct Screen *screen,
                                  struct ESnapWorkArea *buf, ULONG count,
                                  ULONG *needed);

   FUNCTION
        A tiler lays windows out per work area, and it needs an identity
        for one that survives what a struct Screen pointer does not: the
        Workbench screen is closed and reopened by a screen-mode change,
        and comes back at another address. So an area has an id: a public
        screen is known by its name and keeps its id for as long as the
        library is loaded, a private screen is known by its address for
        as long as it stays open. Same id, same area, as far as the
        library can tell. Ids are never reused.

        Up to count entries are written to buf; *needed receives how many
        there are in all, so a short buffer shows as *needed > count.

   INPUTS
        screen - the screen, or NULL for the frontmost
        buf    - where the entries go
        count  - how many entries buf has room for
        needed - receives how many areas there are

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS (needed NULL, or buf NULL with a
                count) / ES_ERR_STALE (no such screen) /
                ES_ERR_NO_MEMORY (no room left for another screen's id;
                sixteen are kept).

   NOTES
        One area per screen for now: monitor reads 0. A screen spanning
        several displays (MorphOS 3.20) is the case this call exists for,
        and it will report one area per display, each with its monitor,
        once the library can tell them apart; a client written against
        this shape needs no change for that day, only a bigger buffer.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_QueryScreenArea(), ESnap_CreateGroup()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_RegisterClient   edgesnap.library/ESnap_RegisterClient

   NAME
        ESnap_RegisterClient -- say what role a client plays (V2.10)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_RegisterClient(name, wants, client_out)
        D0                           A0    D0     A1

        LONG ESnap_RegisterClient(const char *name, ULONG wants,
                                  ULONG *client_out);

   FUNCTION
        A tiler and the commodity run at the same time, and neither may
        switch the other off. A client says what it is, once, from the
        task that will make its calls:

          ES_CL_ENGINE  "I own the interactive drag engine". One holder
                        at a time; a second asker gets ES_ERR_IN_USE.
                        While a holder exists, ESnap_FeedInput,
                        ESnap_FeedMotion and ESnap_ResetInput are taken
                        from its task only and ignored from any other,
                        and its ESnap_Enable is the engine's switch.
          ES_CL_LAYOUT  "I own the layout of the groups I create". Any
                        number of holders. Its ESnap_Enable switches
                        only its own layout on and off, which is what
                        its groups honour; the engine is not touched.

        ESnap_SnapWindow, ESnap_UnsnapWindow, ESnap_PlaceWindow,
        ESnap_PlaceWindowsA and every query stay open to everyone,
        registered or not. A caller that never registers keeps the
        behaviour it always had.

   INPUTS
        name       - for logs, or NULL
        wants      - one or both roles
        client_out - receives the id

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS / ES_ERR_IN_USE /
                ES_ERR_NO_MEMORY.

   NOTES
        Registering again from the same task changes its roles and
        returns the same id. A registration belongs to the task that made
        it and is dropped once that task is gone, the same rule that lets
        windows go stale: the id is never reused.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_UnregisterClient(), ESnap_Enable(), ESnap_CreateGroup()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_ResetInput           edgesnap.library/ESnap_ResetInput

   NAME
        ESnap_ResetInput -- abandon any drag being tracked (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        ESnap_ResetInput(report)
                         A0

        void ESnap_ResetInput(struct ESnapReport *report);

   FUNCTION
        Abandon any tracking: the frontend was disabled, or is quitting.

   INPUTS
        report - where the outcome goes

   RESULT
        None. The report asks for the preview frame to be erased.

   NOTES
        From 2.10, while a client holds ES_CL_ENGINE, calls from any
        other task are ignored.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_FeedInput(), --frontend--

edgesnap.library/ESnap_SetGroupOptionsA

   NAME
        ESnap_SetGroupOptionsA -- the gap and margins of a group (V2.20)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_SetGroupOptionsA(group, tags)
        D0                             D0     A0

        LONG ESnap_SetGroupOptionsA(ULONG group, const struct TagItem *tags);

   FUNCTION
        The global margins (ES_OPT_Margin*) are the user's; a tiler wants
        room between the cells of its layout and around it, its own for
        each group. This call keeps them with the group, and
        ESnap_PlaceWindow and ESnap_PlaceWindowsA take them off a cell
        when asked with ES_PF_CELL: a side at or beyond the edge of the
        usable area gives up the margin of that side, a side shared with
        another cell half the gap, so that two neighbours leave exactly
        the gap between them (an odd pixel goes to the right or bottom
        cell's side).

   INPUTS
        group - the group's id
        tags  - a tag list of the options below

   TAGS
        ES_GO_Gap (LONG, 0..ES_GO_GAP_MAX)
            Room between two cells.

        ES_GO_Margin (LONG, 0..ES_GO_MARGIN_MAX)
            All four margins at once, applied before the per-side tags of
            the same call.

        ES_GO_MarginLeft, ES_GO_MarginTop, ES_GO_MarginRight,
        ES_GO_MarginBottom (LONG, 0..ES_GO_MARGIN_MAX)
            The margin of one side.

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS / ES_ERR_REJECTED (not the
                owner's task) / ES_ERR_STALE (no such group).

   NOTES
        All 0 when the group is made. A change moves no window: it
        applies from the next placement with ES_PF_CELL. Unknown tags are
        ignored; a value out of range changes nothing and answers
        ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS.

        From 2.24 a seam dragged between two of the group's windows keeps
        the gap they have. This header said so from 2.20, which was a
        mistake: up to 2.23 a drag closed a gap of up to twelve pixels,
        and found no seam across a wider one.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_PlaceWindow(), ESnap_QueryDividers(), ESnap_SetOptionsA()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_SetOptionsA         edgesnap.library/ESnap_SetOptionsA

   NAME
        ESnap_SetOptionsA -- change the global options (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_SetOptionsA(tags)
        D0                        A0

        LONG ESnap_SetOptionsA(const struct TagItem *tags);

   FUNCTION
        Set the global options. They apply to future interactions. All
        tags of a call are applied together, and a bad value changes
        nothing.

   INPUTS
        tags - a tag list of the options below

   TAGS
        ES_OPT_EdgePx (LONG, 1..200, default 12)
            How close to an edge the pointer must come, in pixels.

        ES_OPT_CornerDiv (LONG, 0..16, default 0)
            0 or 1: a corner only where the pointer is pushed into it.
            2 to 16: a band at each end of the side edges, the usable
            height divided by this. With corners of no length the top
            edge is the screen's title bar, so a window that sits at the
            top is not in the maximize zone when it is dragged. Up to
            2.18 only 2..16 were accepted and 4 was the default.

        ES_OPT_DragMinPx (LONG, 1..200, default 4)
            How far the pointer must travel before it counts as a drag.

        ES_OPT_MarginLeft, ES_OPT_MarginTop, ES_OPT_MarginRight,
        ES_OPT_MarginBottom (LONG, 0..2000, default 0)
            Extra space left free at that edge, applied on top of what
            dock detection finds.

        ES_OPT_PanelDetect (BOOL, default TRUE)
            Reserve the strips of docks and panels (AmiDock, Ambient
            panels) out of the usable area.

        ES_OPT_PanelMargin (LONG, 0..200, default 8)
            Breathing room left around a detected panel.

        ES_OPT_Zones (ULONG, default ES_ZONEMASK_ALL)
            Which zones react to a drag, as a mask of ES_ZONEBIT(). Bits
            outside ES_ZONEMASK_ALL are dropped.

        ES_OPT_Preview (BOOL, default TRUE)
            Outline where the window will land while dragging.

        ES_OPT_BypassQual (LONG, default ES_QUAL_NONE)
            The qualifier that lets a window be dragged past the zones:
            ES_QUAL_NONE, ES_QUAL_ALT, ES_QUAL_CTRL or ES_QUAL_SHIFT.
            With it held, a snapped window dragged away also keeps its
            snapped size.

        ES_OPT_CycleSizes (BOOL, default TRUE) (V2.13)
            The same side asked for again gives two thirds of the width,
            then one third, then a half again. FALSE: always a half.

        ES_OPT_Animate (BOOL, default TRUE) (V2.13)
            A snap and a restore move the window in a few steps instead
            of one. Every step asks the application to redraw itself, so
            on a slow machine or with a large window the plain jump looks
            better. Placement by a client is never animated. The default
            was FALSE in 2.13 and 2.14.

        ES_OPT_DragRestore (BOOL, default TRUE) (V2.24)
            A window snapped to a zone, dragged away and let go outside
            every zone, gets back its size from before the snap, under
            the pointer. A drag is recognised from the bar press or, from
            2.25, from the window following the pointer. The pointer's
            distance from its start must still meet the threshold set by
            ES_OPT_DragMinPx in either axis; the window's box need not
            move.

        ES_OPT_PushPx (LONG, 0..200, default 24) (V2.26)
            A window held flush with an edge (it may not leave the
            screen, or meets AmigaOS 4's edge resistance) keeps the
            pointer back; this much of the mouse's own travel pushing on
            against that edge counts as the pointer being on it. 0 turns
            the push off: only the pointer reaching the edge makes a
            zone.

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS (tags is NULL, or a value is out
                of range).

   NOTES
        Unknown tags are ignored, so a library older than a tag keeps its
        own behaviour for it. The one exception is ES_OPT_CornerDiv with
        0 or 1: a library older than 2.19 refuses it, and every other tag
        of the same call with it, so a client checks the revision before
        it sends them.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_Enable(), ESnap_SetGroupOptionsA()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_SnapWindow           edgesnap.library/ESnap_SnapWindow

   NAME
        ESnap_SnapWindow -- put a window in a zone (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_SnapWindow(win, zone)
        D0                       A0   D0

        LONG ESnap_SnapWindow(struct Window *win, ULONG zone);

   FUNCTION
        Snap win to zone, and record its geometry from before the snap
        for ESnap_UnsnapWindow. Honors the window's own size limits: the
        result stays anchored to the zone's outer edge when clamped, and
        a centred window stays centred.

   INPUTS
        win  - the window
        zone - ES_ZONE_LEFT to ES_ZONE_MAX, or ES_ZONE_CENTRE from 2.9:
               the window's own size in the middle of the usable area,
               cut down to that area when it is larger than it

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_BAD_ARGS (bad zone) / ES_ERR_STALE /
                ES_ERR_REJECTED (unsnappable or excluded window) /
                ES_ERR_UNSUPPORTED / ES_ERR_NO_MEMORY (registry full;
                the snap is NOT performed - restore must never silently
                become impossible).

   NOTES
        From 2.8 asking for the side a window already occupies walks a
        width cycle: half, two thirds, one third (ES_OPT_CycleSizes,
        2.13, switches it off). A client that wants the same geometry
        every time passes the rectangle itself, through
        ESnap_PlaceWindow.

        From 2.12 a window of a group locked for the caller answers
        ES_ERR_REJECTED.

        From 2.15 the window glides into place unless ES_OPT_Animate is
        FALSE, and the call can wait for the glide (--background--,
        CONTEXT).

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_UnsnapWindow(), ESnap_QueryWindow(), ESnap_PlaceWindow(),
        ESnap_SetOptionsA()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_UnregisterClient

   NAME
        ESnap_UnregisterClient -- give a role up (V2.10)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_UnregisterClient(client)
        D0                             D0

        LONG ESnap_UnregisterClient(ULONG client);

   FUNCTION
        Drop a registration, with its roles. The client's groups die
        with it.

   INPUTS
        client - the id ESnap_RegisterClient gave

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_STALE for an id nobody holds.

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_RegisterClient()

edgesnap.library/ESnap_UnsnapWindow       edgesnap.library/ESnap_UnsnapWindow

   NAME
        ESnap_UnsnapWindow -- give a window back its old box (V2)

   SYNOPSIS
        error = ESnap_UnsnapWindow(win)
        D0                         A0

        LONG ESnap_UnsnapWindow(struct Window *win);

   FUNCTION
        Restore win to its geometry from before the snap, or the first
        placement, and drop its snap state.

   INPUTS
        win - the window

   RESULT
        error - ES_OK / ES_ERR_STALE / ES_ERR_NOT_SNAPPED /
                ES_ERR_CHANGED (the window was moved or resized
                independently since the snap: state dropped, geometry
                untouched).

   NOTES
        From 2.24 a snapped window the user drags away gets that size
        back, under the pointer, and loses its snap state the same way
        (ES_OPT_DragRestore).

   SEE ALSO
        ESnap_SnapWindow(), ESnap_PlaceWindow()
